    "reading": {
        "body": "Utah's 21 tracked data centers carry a combined 39 MW of documented capacity, a figure that almost certainly understates true load because only three of the twenty-one facilities have megawatt figures on record.\n\nThe geographic pattern is tight. Bluffdale, in Salt Lake County, anchors the state's largest concentration, where DataBank operates at least four facilities on what appears to be a single campus along Pony Express Road. Of the three facilities with power data, DataBank SLC6 alone accounts for 22 MW, more than half the recorded state total, with SLC5 at 10 MW and SLC3 at 7 MW. Meta holds five adjacent parcels in Utah County, none with power figures attached. That clustering around two nodes, Bluffdale and the Utah County Meta campus, reflects the land-and-power logic common to data center siting: once grid interconnection and water rights are secured for one building, neighboring parcels become cheaper to develop.\n\nThe 39 MW total is a floor, not a ceiling. Eighteen facilities report no capacity at all, and several major operators including Flexential, Verizon, eBay, and Lumen appear in the record without any megawatt or square-footage data. The data does not cover power source, grid operator, or interconnection queue position, so nothing here speaks to whether Utah's grid can absorb further AI-driven load growth. What it does confirm is that significant colocation and hyperscale infrastructure exists in the state, concentrated in two counties, with measured capacity well below what the full facility list would imply.",
